\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Finite-free coordinates for split factorization}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring. For the
[triangular factorization](fdip-001U) on #{M=X\oplus Y} with
#{C:Y\to Y} invertible, suppose the summands are finite free and
choose finite bases, with coordinate isomorphisms
#{c_X:X\to R^I} and #{c_Y:Y\to R^J}. The 4 arrays of the
[split components](fdip-001T), in row-by-column order, are
##{\begin{aligned}
A_c&=c_XAc_X^{-1},&B_c&=c_XBc_Y^{-1},\\
E_c&=c_YEc_X^{-1},&C_c&=c_YCc_Y^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
The eliminated array has inverse #{c_YC^{-1}c_Y^{-1}}.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.fromBlocks_eq_of_invertible₂₂}
gives the corresponding matrix factorization over a commutative ring
with finite coordinate index types. Its 4 arguments are
#{(A_c,B_c,E_c,C_c)}, and its invertibility assumption is on
#{C_c}. The residual array is #{A_c-B_cC_c^{-1}E_c}, the coordinate
form of the [split Schur map](fdip-001T). This declaration supports
the factorization; the [inverse](fdip-001V) and
[kernel](fdip-001W) statements have their own proofs.}
